    fmaPreferredName: 
  - partName: Scapular spinal part of deltoid muscle.l
    systemName: Muscular System
    partType: Skeletal Muscle
    description: "The deltoid is a thick, multipennate muscle cap that drapes over the glenohumeral joint. It arises from the lateral third of the clavicle, acromion, and scapular spine, converging distally to insert onto the deltoid tuberosity of the humerus."
    function: "The central acromial fibers are the primary motor for arm abduction between 15° and 90°; the anterior clavicular fibers flex and medially rotate the arm; the posterior spinal fibers extend and laterally rotate the arm."
    location: "Forms the prominent rounded contour of the shoulder; innervated by the axillary nerve (C5, C6). Left side (bilateral structure)."
    fmaId: 
    fmaPreferredName: 
  - partName: Scapular spinal part of deltoid muscle.r
    systemName: Muscular System
    partType: Skeletal Muscle
    description: "The deltoid is a thick, multipennate muscle cap that drapes over the glenohumeral joint. It arises from the lateral third of the clavicle, acromion, and scapular spine, converging distally to insert onto the deltoid tuberosity of the humerus."
    function: "The central acromial fibers are the primary motor for arm abduction between 15° and 90°; the anterior clavicular fibers flex and medially rotate the arm; the posterior spinal fibers extend and laterally rotate the arm."
    location: "Forms the prominent rounded contour of the shoulder; innervated by the axillary nerve (C5, C6). Right side (bilateral structure)."
    fmaId: 
    fmaPreferredName: 
  - partName: Semimembranosus bursa.l
    systemName: Joints & Articulations
    partType: Synovial Bursa
    description: "An articulated junction (Semimembranosus bursa) where adjacent skeletal elements meet, lined with smooth articular hyaline cartilage and enclosed by a synovial fluid-filled capsule."
    function: "Permits controlled mechanical degrees of freedom (flexion, extension, rotation, or gliding) while distributing biomechanical compressive loads."
    location: "Positioned between articulating bones in the human body. Left side (bilateral structure)."
