    function: "Conducts bidirectional electrophysiological signals - relays motor impulses from the spinal cord to target muscles and sensory feedback (proprioception, pain, temperature) back to the central nervous system."
    location: "Courses within the neurovascular bundle of the forearm (antebrachial region). Left side (bilateral structure)."
    fmaId: 
    fmaPreferredName: 
  - partName: Ulnar nerve.r
    systemName: Nervous System
    partType: Peripheral Nerve
    description: "A peripheral neural cable (Ulnar nerve) composed of myelinated and unmyelinated nerve fibers bundled into fascicles by endoneurium, perineurium, and epineurium."
    function: "Conducts bidirectional electrophysiological signals - relays motor impulses from the spinal cord to target muscles and sensory feedback (proprioception, pain, temperature) back to the central nervous system."
    location: "Courses within the neurovascular bundle of the forearm (antebrachial region). Right side (bilateral structure)."
    fmaId: 
    fmaPreferredName: 
  - partName: Uvula of vermis
    systemName: Skeletal System
    partType: Bony Structure
    description: "A rigid skeletal organ (Uvula of vermis) constructed from an outer dense cortical bone layer, internal spongy trabecular cancellous bone, periosteum, and bone marrow."
    function: "Provides structural body scaffolding, mechanical lever arms for muscle action, hematopoiesis (blood cell production), and a systemic metabolic reservoir for calcium and phosphate."
    location: "Constitutes an integral part of the skeletal framework in the human body. Midline anatomical structure on the central body axis."
    fmaId: 
    fmaPreferredName: 
  - partName: Vagus nerve (X).l
    systemName: Nervous System
    partType: Cranial Nerve
    description: "The vagus nerve (Cranial Nerve X) is the longest cranial nerve, containing approximately 80% visceral sensory afferents and 20% general visceral parasympathetic efferents."
    function: "Carries essential parasympathetic autonomic tone to decelerate heart rate, constrict bronchioles, stimulate digestive gastrointestinal peristalsis and acid secretion, and innervate laryngeal muscles for vocal phonation."
    location: "Exits the posterior cranial fossa via the jugular foramen, descends within the carotid sheath in the neck, traverses the mediastinum, and enters the abdomen through the esophageal hiatus. Left side (bilateral structure)."
    fmaId: 
    fmaPreferredName: 
